(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["phy-12-05"] = {"phy-12-05-m01":{"idea":"Simple harmonic motion (SHM) is motion where the pull back to the centre grows with the distance from the centre.","steps":["The defining rule is a = −ω²x.","Here x is the displacement from the mean position and ω is a constant.","The size of a is proportional to x.","The minus sign says a always points towards the mean position."],"others":"Acceleration proportional to speed along the velocity would make the particle speed up forever. Constant acceleration towards the centre does not depend on x. Acceleration directed away from the centre would push the particle out and it would never oscillate.","trap":"Two things must hold together: proportional to x, and directed towards the centre.","answer":"proportional to its displacement from the mean position and directed towards it"},"phy-12-05-m02":{"idea":"SHM needs a sine or cosine of a time term that grows steadily with t.","steps":["For x = A sin(ωt + φ), the acceleration is a = d²x/dt² = −ω²x.","This is exactly the SHM rule.","x = A e^(ωt) grows without limit.","x = Aωt is straight-line motion, and A sin(ωt²) has a changing frequency."],"others":"The exponential grows forever and never returns. Aωt is uniform motion in one direction. A sin(ωt²) oscillates faster and faster, so a is not proportional to x.","trap":"A real SHM equation has t only inside the sine, and only to the first power.","answer":"x = A sin(ωt + φ)"},"phy-12-05-m03":{"idea":"Period is the time for one full oscillation. In that time, the phase ωt grows by 2π.","steps":["One oscillation means ωT = 2π.","So T = 2π/ω.","Frequency is f = 1/T = ω/2π.","Check units: ω in rad/s gives T in seconds."],"others":"ω/2π is actually the frequency f, not the period. ω²/2π has the wrong units. 2πω has the units of rad per second, which is not a time.","trap":"T = 2π/ω and f = ω/2π are inverse of each other.","answer":"T = 2π/ω"},"phy-12-05-m04":{"idea":"The speed in SHM is greatest where the particle passes the centre.","steps":["The speed at displacement x is v = ω√(A² − x²).","This is largest when x = 0.","Then v = ω√(A²) = Aω.","So v_max = Aω, at the mean position."],"others":"Aω² is the unit of acceleration, not speed. At the extreme position the particle stops for an instant, so its speed is zero. A/ω has the wrong units.","trap":"Speed is largest at the middle, acceleration is largest at the ends.","answer":"Aω, at the mean position"},"phy-12-05-m05":{"idea":"The acceleration in SHM is largest where the particle is farthest from the centre.","steps":["a = −ω²x, so the size of a is ω²|x|.","|x| is largest at the extreme positions, where |x| = A.","So a_max = Aω².","At the mean position, x = 0, so a = 0."],"others":"Aω has the units of speed, not acceleration. At the mean position the acceleration is zero, so the other two choices that name the mean position cannot be a maximum there.","trap":"Check units: m × (rad/s)² gives m/s².","answer":"Aω², at the extreme positions"},"phy-12-05-m06":{"idea":"Use the speed formula with x = A/2.","steps":["v = ω√(A² − x²).","Put x = A/2: A² − A²/4 = 3A²/4.","v = ω × (A√3/2).","So v = (√3/2) Aω, about 0.87 Aω."],"others":"(3/4)Aω forgets to take the square root of 3/4. Aω/2 treats the speed as falling in a straight line with x. Aω/√2 is the speed when x = A/√2, not A/2.","trap":"Remember the square root: √(3/4) = √3/2.","answer":"(√3/2) Aω"},"phy-12-05-m07":{"idea":"The total energy of a harmonic oscillator is fixed. It depends on amplitude, not on position.","steps":["At the extreme position, the speed is zero and all energy is potential.","Potential energy there is ½kA², with k = mω².","So E = ½ m ω² A².","At other positions kinetic and potential energy change, but their sum stays the same."],"others":"½mω²x² is only the potential energy at one position x. mω²A² is twice the right value. ½mωA² has ω to the first power, so its units are wrong.","trap":"Total energy is ½mω²A²: it grows with the square of the amplitude.","answer":"½mω²A², the same at every position"},"phy-12-05-m08":{"idea":"Energy keeps changing form between kinetic and potential in SHM.","steps":["At the mean position, x = 0.","Potential energy is ½kx² = 0, the minimum.","All the energy is therefore kinetic, so KE is at its maximum.","The speed is greatest here, which agrees."],"others":"Both being maximum would make the total more than the total energy. Kinetic zero and potential maximum describes the extreme positions. Both being zero would mean the energy vanishes.","trap":"Mean position: all kinetic. Extreme positions: all potential.","answer":"the kinetic energy is maximum and the potential energy is minimum"},"phy-12-05-m09":{"idea":"Find the position where the two energies are equal by setting them equal.","steps":["KE = ½k(A² − x²) and PE = ½kx².","Set them equal: A² − x² = x².","2x² = A², so x² = A²/2.","x = ±A/√2, about ±0.707A."],"others":"At zero displacement all energy is kinetic. ±A/√3 and ±A/2 do not give equal energies when put in the formulas: at ±A/2 the kinetic energy is three times the potential.","trap":"Each energy is half of the total at x = ±A/√2.","answer":"±A/√2"},"phy-12-05-m10":{"idea":"A spring supplies a restoring force −kx, so the mass moves in SHM with ω² = k/m.","steps":["Newton's second law: m a = −k x, so a = −(k/m) x.","Compare with a = −ω²x: ω² = k/m.","T = 2π/ω.","So T = 2π√(m/k)."],"others":"2π√(mk) has k in the wrong place: a stiffer spring would give a slower motion. √(m/k)/2π has the 2π inverted. 2π√(k/m) is the inverse of the right result and describes 1/T instead of T.","trap":"A heavier mass is slower; a stiffer spring is faster.","answer":"2π√(m/k)"},"phy-12-05-m11":{"idea":"The period depends on the square root of the mass.","steps":["T = 2π√(m/k), so T is proportional to √m.","The mass becomes 4m.","New T = 2π√(4m/k) = 2 × 2π√(m/k).","So the period doubles."],"others":"Unchanged would mean T does not depend on m, which is false for a spring. Half as long is the reverse direction. Four times ignores the square root.","trap":"Four times the mass gives twice the period, not four times.","answer":"twice as long"},"phy-12-05-m12":{"idea":"For small swings, a pendulum is in SHM with ω² = g/L.","steps":["The restoring force along the arc is about −mgθ.","The arc displacement is x = Lθ, so the force is −(mg/L)x.","Then ω² = g/L.","T = 2π/ω = 2π√(L/g)."],"others":"2π√(Lg) would make a longer pendulum swing faster. √(L/g)/2π has the 2π in the wrong place. 2π√(g/L) is the inverse of the right result and gets longer for a shorter pendulum.","trap":"The mass does not appear in the period.","answer":"2π√(L/g)"},"phy-12-05-m13":{"idea":"The period of a pendulum depends on the square root of its length.","steps":["T = 2π√(L/g), so T is proportional to √L.","The length becomes 4L.","New T = 2π√(4L/g) = 2 × 2π√(L/g).","The period doubles."],"others":"Four times ignores the square root. Sixteen times squares the factor instead of taking its root. Half as long would need the pendulum to be shorter.","trap":"For a pendulum, length 4 times gives period 2 times.","answer":"twice as long"},"phy-12-05-m14":{"idea":"A second's pendulum takes 2 s for one full swing there and back. Use the period formula to find its length.","steps":["T = 2π√(L/g), so L = gT²/(4π²).","g = 9.8 m/s² and T = 2 s.","L = 9.8 × 4/(4 × 9.87) = 39.2/39.48.","L = 0.993 m, so L is about 1 m."],"others":"2 m and 4 m are too long: they would give periods of about 2.8 s and 4 s. 0.25 m is too short and would give a period of about 1 s.","trap":"Rule of thumb: 1 m gives about 2 s on Earth.","answer":"1 m"},"phy-12-05-m15":{"idea":"In an accelerating frame, a pendulum feels a different effective gravity.","steps":["When the lift accelerates upwards with a, the effective gravity is g_eff = g + a.","T = 2π√(L/g_eff).","g_eff is larger than g.","So T is smaller: the pendulum swings faster."],"others":"Longer, with larger effective gravity, contradicts the formula: larger g gives a shorter T. Longer with smaller effective gravity is the case for a lift accelerating downwards. The same period would hold only if g did not change, but it does.","trap":"Lift up: g + a, shorter period. Lift down: g − a, longer period.","answer":"shorter, because the effective gravity is larger"},"phy-12-05-m16":{"idea":"A pendulum clock counts swings. If each swing takes longer, the clock falls behind.","steps":["T = 2π√(L/g).","On the Moon g is smaller, so T is larger.","Each swing takes longer than a second.","Fewer swings are counted in an hour, so the clock runs slow."],"others":"Running fast would need shorter swings, which needs a larger g. The mass does not matter, but g does, so correct time is wrong. A shorter period would also make the clock fast, which is the opposite of the result.","trap":"Longer period means a slow clock.","answer":"run slow, because the period of the pendulum is longer"},"phy-12-05-m17":{"idea":"Springs side by side share the load, so they act as one stiffer spring.","steps":["For parallel springs, k_eff = k + k = 2k.","T = 2π√(m/k_eff) = 2π√(m/2k).","This is (1/√2) × 2π√(m/k).","So the period becomes T₁/√2."],"others":"Twice as long would need a weaker spring. √2 times as long also needs a weaker spring. Half as long would need k to become four times larger, but it only doubles.","trap":"Parallel springs: k adds. Series springs: 1/k adds.","answer":"1/√2 times as long"},"phy-12-05-m18":{"idea":"A damped oscillator loses energy to its surroundings, for example to air resistance.","steps":["The resistive force acts against the motion.","It does negative work on the oscillator.","So the total energy falls with time.","Energy depends on A², so the amplitude falls too."],"others":"Increasing amplitude would need energy to be added. Constant amplitude is only for an undamped oscillator. A sudden jump to zero is not how friction works; the amplitude decays smoothly.","trap":"The decay is often exponential: A = A₀ e^(−bt/2m).","answer":"decreases with time, as energy is lost to the surroundings"},"phy-12-05-m19":{"idea":"Resonance is the large response of a system to a push at the right frequency.","steps":["A system has its own natural frequency.","If it is pushed at that same frequency, each push adds to the motion.","Energy is fed in at the best rate.","So the amplitude becomes largest, limited only by damping."],"others":"The size of the driving force alone does not decide the response. A driving frequency of twice the natural frequency is not the resonance condition. Maximum damping reduces the peak instead of creating it.","trap":"A child on a swing is pushed at the swing's own frequency.","answer":"the driving frequency equals the natural frequency of the system"},"phy-12-05-m20":{"idea":"Differentiating displacement gives velocity, and sine becomes cosine.","steps":["Let x = A sin ωt.","Then v = dx/dt = Aω cos ωt = Aω sin(ωt + π/2).","So velocity is the same wave shifted by π/2.","Velocity leads displacement by π/2 (90°)."],"others":"π would mean the velocity is opposite to the displacement; that is the acceleration. 0 would mean the two are in step, but the velocity is zero when displacement is largest. π/4 does not come from any one differentiation.","trap":"One derivative: π/2 shift. Two derivatives: π shift.","answer":"π/2"},"phy-12-05-m21":{"idea":"The acceleration is the second derivative of displacement, which flips its sign.","steps":["a = −ω²x.","The minus sign means a is always opposite to x.","Two waves that are opposite have a phase difference of π.","So the phase difference is π."],"others":"π/2 is the phase difference between displacement and velocity. 0 would make a point the same way as x, but it points the other way. 3π/2 would be the phase of the acceleration relative to velocity in the wrong direction.","trap":"Acceleration and displacement are always in opposite directions.","answer":"π"},"phy-12-05-m22":{"idea":"The shadow of a point on a circle, falling on a diameter, moves back and forth in SHM.","steps":["A particle moves around a circle of radius R at constant angular speed ω.","Its projection on a diameter is x = R cos(ωt + φ).","This is a cosine of a term linear in time.","So the projection is SHM with amplitude R and the same ω."],"others":"The projection is not uniform: it slows near the ends of the diameter. It is not uniformly accelerated either; its acceleration changes with x. The amplitude is the radius, not the whole diameter.","trap":"Amplitude is the radius, not the diameter.","answer":"simple harmonic motion with the radius as the amplitude"},"phy-12-05-m23":{"idea":"Compare the given equation with x = A sin(ωt + φ) to read off ω.","steps":["The standard form is x = A sin(ωt + φ).","The given equation has ωt = 4πt, so ω = 4π rad/s.","f = ω/2π = 4π/2π.","f = 2 Hz."],"others":"13 Hz adds the numbers in the bracket, which has no meaning. 1 Hz would need ω = 2π. 8 Hz forgets to divide by 2π and treats 4π/π as the frequency.","trap":"The constant π/6 is the starting phase and does not affect frequency.","answer":"2 Hz"},"phy-12-05-m24":{"idea":"Distance is the total length of path; displacement only compares start and end.","steps":["Start at the mean position and move to +A: distance A.","Return to the mean position: another A, so 2A so far.","Go to −A and come back: another 2A.","Total distance is 4A, while displacement is zero."],"others":"A is only a quarter of a cycle. 2A is only half a cycle. Zero is the displacement for a full cycle, not the distance.","trap":"Distance in one cycle: 4A. Displacement in one cycle: 0.","answer":"4A"},"phy-12-05-m25":{"idea":"At the end points of the motion, the particle turns around.","steps":["At x = ±A, the particle stops for an instant, so speed is zero.","The restoring force is largest there, since |x| is largest.","a = −ω²x has its largest size at |x| = A.","So speed is zero and acceleration is maximum."],"others":"Both maximum is not possible: speed and acceleration peak at different places. Speed maximum and acceleration zero describes the mean position. Both zero cannot happen, since the restoring force is largest here.","trap":"When one is at maximum, the other is zero.","answer":"the speed is zero and the acceleration is maximum"},"phy-12-05-m26":{"idea":"The period of a vertical spring-mass system does not depend on gravity.","steps":["T = 2π√(m/k).","The formula has only the mass and the spring constant.","Gravity only shifts the rest position of the mass downwards.","So the period on the Moon is the same as on Earth."],"others":"Shorter and longer would need g in the formula, and it is not there. Zero is not possible, because the spring and the mass still oscillate.","trap":"A pendulum depends on g, but a spring-mass system does not.","answer":"the same as on Earth"},"phy-12-05-m27":{"idea":"A pendulum is in SHM only if the restoring force is proportional to the displacement. This holds for small angles.","steps":["The weight component along the arc is −mg sinθ.","For small θ (in radians), sinθ ≈ θ.","So the force is about −mgθ.","This is proportional to the displacement, so the motion is SHM."],"others":"−mgθ² grows too slowly and is not what the geometry gives. −mg cosθ is the component along the string, which balances the tension. −mg for any angle is the full weight and ignores the geometry.","trap":"Small angle approximation: sinθ ≈ θ.","answer":"−mgθ, where θ is the angle in radians"},"phy-12-05-m28":{"idea":"The unit of k follows from the spring force law F = kx.","steps":["F = kx, so k = F/x.","F is in newton and x is in metre.","So k is in N/m.","A stiffer spring has a larger k."],"others":"kg/s is the unit of a mass flow rate. N m is the unit of work or torque. N/s is the unit of a rate of change of force.","trap":"Check: N/m = kg/s². The unit kg/s is not the same.","answer":"newton per metre (N/m)"},"phy-12-05-n01":{"idea":"Angular frequency is 2π divided by the period.","steps":["ω = 2π/T.","T = 0.5 s.","ω = 2π/0.5 = 4π.","ω = 12.57 rad/s."],"answer":"12.57 rad/s"},"phy-12-05-n02":{"idea":"Maximum speed in SHM is amplitude times angular frequency.","steps":["ω = 2πf = 2π × 5 = 10π rad/s.","v_max = Aω.","v_max = 0.05 × 10π = 0.5π.","v_max = 1.57 m/s."],"trap":"Use f in hertz to find ω first.","answer":"1.57 m/s"},"phy-12-05-n03":{"idea":"Maximum acceleration is amplitude times ω squared.","steps":["a_max = Aω².","A = 0.02 m and ω = 10 rad/s.","ω² = 100.","a_max = 0.02 × 100 = 2.0 m/s²."],"answer":"2.0 m/s²"},"phy-12-05-n04":{"idea":"The period of a spring-mass system depends on mass and spring constant.","steps":["T = 2π√(m/k).","m = 0.5 kg and k = 200 N/m.","m/k = 0.0025, and √0.0025 = 0.05.","T = 2π × 0.05 = 0.314 s."],"answer":"0.314 s"},"phy-12-05-n05":{"idea":"The period of a simple pendulum depends on length and gravity.","steps":["T = 2π√(L/g).","L = 0.64 m and g = 10 m/s², so L/g = 0.064.","√0.064 = 0.2530.","T = 2π × 0.2530 = 1.59 s."],"answer":"1.59 s"},"phy-12-05-n06":{"idea":"Total energy in SHM is the potential energy at the extreme position.","steps":["E = ½kA².","k = 100 N/m and A = 0.1 m, so A² = 0.01 m².","E = ½ × 100 × 0.01.","E = 0.5 J."],"answer":"0.5 J"},"phy-12-05-n07":{"idea":"Use the speed formula for a given displacement.","steps":["v = ω√(A² − x²).","A² = 0.01 and x² = 0.06² = 0.0036.","A² − x² = 0.0064, and √0.0064 = 0.08.","v = 10 × 0.08 = 0.8 m/s."],"trap":"Check: 0.8 is less than Aω = 1.0 m/s, as it must be.","answer":"0.8 m/s"},"phy-12-05-n08":{"idea":"Springs side by side add their force constants.","steps":["k = k₁ + k₂ = 100 + 300 = 400 N/m.","T = 2π√(m/k).","m/k = 1/400, so √(m/k) = 1/20.","T = 2π/20 = 0.314 s."],"answer":"0.314 s"},"phy-12-05-n09":{"idea":"Rearrange the pendulum formula to find the length.","steps":["T = 2π√(L/g), so L = gT²/(4π²).","g = 9.8 m/s² and T = 2 s, so gT² = 39.2.","4π² = 39.478.","L = 39.2/39.478 = 0.993 m."],"answer":"0.993 m"},"phy-12-05-n10":{"idea":"In an upward-accelerating lift, the effective gravity is larger.","steps":["g_eff = g + a = 10 + 2.5 = 12.5 m/s².","T = 2π√(L/g_eff).","L/g_eff = 1/12.5 = 0.08, and √0.08 = 0.2828.","T = 2π × 0.2828 = 1.78 s."],"trap":"This is shorter than 2π√(1/10) = 1.99 s, as expected for a larger effective gravity.","answer":"1.78 s"}};
